The buffer solutions used in this work are as follows. Tris-HCl buffer \(20\ \mathrm{mM}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}\ 7.0)\) containing \(\mathrm{NaCl}(140\ \mathrm{mM})\) , KCl \(\left(5\ \mathrm{mM}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) (1 mM) was used to prepare the oligonucleotide solutions. PBS (0.1 M, pH 7.0) solution with \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{HPO}_{4}\) \(\left(10\ \mathrm{mM}\right)\) , \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}(10\ \mathrm{mM})\) and \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) \(\left(2\ \mathrm{mM}\right)\) was employed to prepare TB solution and used as detection buffer in the experiments. Each hairpin DNA (HP) was heated to \(90\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) and cooled before use. Apparatus and measurements
Differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) and cyclic voltammetry (CV) were tested by CHI 660D electrochemical workstation. The microstructure of nanomaterials was characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-4800, Hitachi Instrument, Japan).

Preparation of Co-MOFs

Co-MOFs were prepared through a previous route with some revision.\(^{17}\) 0.2183 g of Co \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)_{2} \cdot 6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}\) and 0.0815 g of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-BDC were mixed into the 4 mL of N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF). After ultrasonicated for 15 min, the above solution was heated in an oil bath \((140\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\). The resulting Co-MOFs were obtained by centrifugation. Finally, the collected particles were heated at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for about \(6\ \mathrm{h}\) for further use.

Preparation of Co-MOFs/PtPd NPs

The Pt-Pd nanoparticles (PtPd NPs) were synthesized according to the reference.\(^{21}\) First, \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(2\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}\) solution and \(40\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(10\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) solution were added into \(2\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.25\ \mathrm{mM}\) CTAB solution. Next, \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\ 0.1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{AA}\) solution was dropped into the above solution under stirring. Then, the obtained mixed solution were transferred into a water bath quickly and heated in \(30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\ \mathrm{h}\). Finally, PtPd NPs were obtained after centrifugation and washed.

Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs were prepared by adding \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of PtPd NPs solution into \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) of Co-MOFs solution and stirred for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). The Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs were obtained by centrifugation. Preparation of Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs/HP3
Firstly, \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of HP3 solution were added into 1 mL Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs and stirred \(16~\mathrm{h}\) under \(4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Secondly, the mixed solution was centrifuged and washed. Finally, the obtained product was dispersed in PBS buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.0)\). The preparation of Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs/HP3 was shown in Scheme 1A. Fabrication of aptasensor

The detection principle for TB is shown in Scheme 1B. First, TBA and its complementary oligonucleotides 1 (DNA s1) were previously hybridized in equal proportions \((20~\upmu\mathrm{L},\,2.5~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) and incubated at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). Then the obtained complex was incubated in \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) different concentrations of TB at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). When the biosensor was incubated with different concentration of TB, different amount of single-stranded DNA s1 was released from the TBA/S1 hybrids in the process.

The recycle process was performed as follows: the released DNA s1 solution \((20~\upmu\mathrm{L})\), HP1 solution \((2~\upmu\mathrm{M},\,10~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) and NEB buffer \((10\times,\,10~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) were mixed and incubated at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(45~\mathrm{min}\). Then, \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) \(2~\mathrm{U/\upmuL}\) Nt.AlWI was added and incubated at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). After incubation, the obtained mixtures were heated at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20~\mathrm{min}\) to terminate the reaction.

Firstly, the cleaned glassy carbon electrode GCE was dipped into \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) solution \((1~\mathrm{wt}~\%)\) and electrodeposited at \(-0.2~\mathrm{V}\) for \(30~\mathrm{seconds}\) to obtain gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) layer. After washed, the GCE was incubated with \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) HP2 solution \((2~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for about \(16~\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. Then, \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) HT solution \((1.0~\mathrm{mM})\) was dropped to block the nonspecific combining sites. Subsequently, \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\)
resultant output DNA s2 and s2' was dropped onto the electrode and incubated for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to unfold the HP2. After that, the electrode was washed and incubated with \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the Co-MOFs/PtPdNPs/HP3 for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\).

# Results and discussion

Characteristics of Co-MOFs

Figure 1A is the SEM image of Co-MOFs with flower-like structures. After PtPd NPs was coated on the Co-MOFs (Figure 1B), intensive highlights represented PtPd NPs could be clearly observed on the surface of Co-MOFs sphere, revealing that the PtPd NPs were successfully captured on Co-MOFs.

